1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:06,157
Many requests, as one call.

2
00:00:06,957 --> 00:00:17,098
A composed request bundles independent requests into one call. Each slot is a complete request, with its own cohort, filters, groups and aggregations.

3
00:00:17,898 --> 00:00:27,613
Our cohort is illustrative: two markets, a hundred and four weeks each of TV spend, search spend and sales. Three slots ask three questions of it.

4
00:00:28,413 --> 00:00:36,784
Response i answers request i. A slot with no label gets request, underscore, and its position: here, request three.

5
00:00:37,584 --> 00:00:44,760
Labels are how overlays and rankings name slots, so two slots with one label are refused before any slot runs.

6
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:53,227
With parallel workers, slots overlap, but the responses still come back in request order, byte for byte the serial run.

7
00:00:54,027 --> 00:01:02,462
A batch saves round-trips, not decode work: every slot opens its own cohort. When one step reads another's rows, that is a chain.

8
00:01:03,262 --> 00:01:12,422
Overlays on the composed request run after every slot has finished. Here, sales by market in twenty twenty-four and in twenty twenty-five.

9
00:01:13,222 --> 00:01:21,081
An index and a delta against the twenty twenty-four slot: north fell to about ninety-five, south to about ninety-six.

10
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:30,124
The fold reads the finished slots and never changes them. With or without the overlays, both slots' responses are byte-identical.

11
00:01:30,924 --> 00:01:37,716
Compared slots must share their shape, so vary the filter or the cohort. The overlays film covers the kinds.

12
00:01:38,516 --> 00:01:47,484
A sweep is one request body plus axes of values. Double braces splice a value as text; a var marker keeps its JSON type.

13
00:01:48,284 --> 00:01:55,545
Grid mode takes every combination: two markets by two years make four ordinary slots, labelled by the pattern.

14
00:01:56,345 --> 00:02:04,417
Zip mode walks the axes in lockstep instead: three bands of TV spend make three slots, and average sales climb with spend.

15
00:02:05,217 --> 00:02:11,540
Predict expands the sweep without running it: the expanded count and the very labels the run will use.

16
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:20,476
The slot limit counts the expanded total, so an oversized sweep is refused before anything runs. Each body decodes strictly.

17
00:02:21,276 --> 00:02:28,175
A placeholder never goes inside an expression. To vary a value an expression reads, sweep a request param.

18
00:02:28,975 --> 00:02:37,068
Now a real search. Each slot adstocks TV with a different decay and fits a regression, holding out the last thirteen weeks.

19
00:02:37,868 --> 00:02:44,191
Rank orders the sweep slots by one number: here, the held-out error. A decay of point six wins.

20
00:02:44,991 --> 00:02:54,791
The ranking reports each slot's value, rank, index and axes. Our generator used point six, and the winner's decay comes back as a number in axes.

21
00:02:55,591 --> 00:03:04,452
The path walks object keys, and picks list elements by name or label, never by index. Order is ascending unless you say otherwise.

22
00:03:05,252 --> 00:03:12,236
A path that names nothing in any slot, here a misspelt R M S E, is refused with a sweep rank path error.

23
00:03:13,036 --> 00:03:20,084
The ranking sits outside the return. It is always emitted and never rounded, even when the slots drop their rows.

24
00:03:20,884 --> 00:03:29,660
A grid multiplies with every axis. Random and L H S modes draw a fixed number of samples from each axis's range instead.

25
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:34,713
Random draws every point independently, so it leaves gaps and clumps.

26
00:03:35,513 --> 00:03:43,628
L H S, a Latin hypercube, cuts each axis into as many strata as samples, and draws each stratum exactly once.

27
00:03:44,428 --> 00:03:52,052
Here, every one of the forty strata on both axes. Random reached twenty-five on one axis and twenty-nine on the other.

28
00:03:52,852 --> 00:04:00,284
The draws come from the spec and a seed, never from the data or the clock. The response echoes the seed as sweep seed.

29
00:04:01,084 --> 00:04:07,577
Pin it as R N G seed and the run replays exactly: the same forty draws, the same ranking.

30
00:04:08,377 --> 00:04:12,054
Set another seed and you get a fresh set. The winner moves a little.

31
00:04:12,854 --> 00:04:21,737
Both times, TV's decay sits near point six, but search's wanders: these data barely tell it. Read the top few, not only the first.

32
00:04:22,537 --> 00:04:30,097
For each does the same on one item inside a request. One window item becomes three, one per zipped decay and label.

33
00:04:30,897 --> 00:04:37,625
Each becomes an ordinary column. The higher the decay, the longer last week's TV carries into this one.

34
00:04:38,425 --> 00:04:45,494
Predict expands it exactly as the run does. It works on windows, features and attributes, by grid or zip.

35
00:04:46,294 --> 00:04:50,889
Inside a sweep body it is refused: put its axes on the sweep instead.

36
00:04:51,689 --> 00:04:57,756
Sweep the holdout start through a request param. A start at week one hundred and five holds out nothing.

37
00:04:58,556 --> 00:05:02,511
That regression fails on its data, and its result carries the error.

38
00:05:03,311 --> 00:05:10,167
A slot failed warning names it, the ranking leaves that slot out with a warning, and the other slots rank as usual.

39
00:05:10,967 --> 00:05:17,332
A fault in the data fails that part alone, and the rest survives. Under Strict, the batch fails instead.

40
00:05:18,132 --> 00:05:24,775
A fault in the request itself, like a misspelt field, refuses the whole batch, and the error names the slot.

41
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:33,818
A banner writes a survey table once. Sections and column groups expand into ordinary crosstab slots, a Total block first.

42
00:05:34,618 --> 00:05:40,173
The response stitches them side by side. This one comes from a small survey of six hundred.

43
00:05:40,973 --> 00:05:47,381
Letters mark a column significantly above another in its own group. The Total block is compared to nothing.

44
00:05:48,181 --> 00:05:55,293
That's Compose today: batches, the overlay fold, sweeps and their rankings, fan-out, and slots that fail alone.

45
00:05:56,093 --> 00:06:05,786
More are added over time, and the manifest lists what your instance offers. Embedders can register their own regressions through options dot extensions.

46
00:06:06,586 --> 00:06:12,119
Next, the same cohort goes further: fit once, bind the winner, and optimize the budget.

47
00:06:12,919 --> 00:06:14,186
Pulse Compose.
